fact WorkItem(id: "WI-982", description: some(value: "`reflect.KB.nonvar` / `reflect.KB.ground` RE-DERIVE the resolver's answer on a\nraw TermId. They must be CARRIER-NEUTRAL -- one owner per question.\n\nFOUND WHILE DRIVING WI-981 (proposal 059 R4). The capture clause flagged\n`anthill.reflect.KB.nonvar` as shadowing `anthill.reflect.nonvar`, and the\nshadow turned out to be the smaller half of the finding: the QUESTION is\nimplemented twice.\n\n  \"is this a variable?\"\n    resolver  resolve.rs:3474 builtin_nonvar -> walk_arg (chases σ) +\n              value_is_unbound_var; carrier-neutral via TermView;\n              THREE-VALUED (Failure / delay / Success)\n    host      anthill-stl/src/reflect/builtins.rs:804 kb_nonvar ->\n              !matches!(interp.kb().get_term(tid), CoreTerm::Var(_));\n              raw TermId; TWO-VALUED Bool\n\n  \"is this ground?\"\n    resolver  resolve.rs:3483 builtin_ground -> value_is_ground, documented as\n              \"the shared core of the ground(?x) builtin and the NAF groundness\n              gate, so neither materializes the goal to a TermId\"; handles\n              Value::Term (chasing σ into subterms), Value::Node occurrences,\n              and value-level logic vars\n    host      builtins.rs:812 kb_ground ->\n              interp.kb().collect_vars(tid).is_empty()\n\nTHREE DIFFERENCES, not one:\n  1. σ-AWARENESS. The resolver chases the substitution; the host copy never\n     consults σ. A term whose vars are all BOUND by σ is ground to one and not\n     ground to the other.\n  2. CARRIER. `expect_term` (builtins.rs:792) accepts ONLY Value::Term -- every\n     other carrier is a hard TypeMismatch. So the host copy cannot see the\n     Value::Node occurrence carrier that value_is_ground handles explicitly.\n  3. VALUEDNESS. delay vs plain Bool.\n\nTHE JUSTIFICATION IS PHASE-SCOPED, AND THE LANGUAGE HAS OTHER PHASES. kb_nonvar's\ndoc says \"Eval-time runs after SLD, so arguments are already grounded where they\nwill be; no DELAY semantics needed here.\" That is true of the post-SLD path and\nfalse of at least two others that are DELIVERED, not planned:\n\n  - COMPILE-TIME MACRO EXPANSION (WI-722 / proposal 043.1). simp_rewrite.rs:788+,\n    in its own words: \"We bind the macro's params to those occurrences as\n    Value::Node -- NOT materialized: the flatten in bridge_op_to_eval is\n    deliberately skipped ... so occurrence structure survives -- and run the body\n    through the WI-625 scratch interpreter.\" Arguments are occurrences carrying\n    pattern variables. Both halves of the assumption fail: not Terms, not ground.\n  - ABSTRACT INTERPRETATION ON SUSPEND (WI-580, design/abstract-interpreter-and-\n    rules.md §3.3, delivered 2026-07-09). `unfold_eq_operand` /\n    body_specialize.rs::folded_call_match case-splits a bodied op-call's body\n    PRECISELY BECAUSE the call is unground.\n\nSo the comment records an assumption about one caller, not an equivalence.\n\nMEASURED, not inferred. Driver in anthill-stl's own `reflect::builtins::tests`\nmodule, where both owners are reachable (anthill-stl depends on anthill-core, so\na test there can build a KB, resolve a goal, AND call the registered host fns --\nan earlier attempt from anthill-core failed only because register_reflect_builtins\nlives in anthill-stl):\n\n  [resolver] ground(5)                 => 1 solution     (\"yes, ground\")\n  [host]     KB.ground(kb, Int(5))     => ERROR: type mismatch: expected Term,\n                                                 got Int64 for KB.ground\n  [host]     KB.ground(kb, Str(\"hi\"))  => ERROR: ... got String\n  [host]     KB.ground(kb, Bool(true)) => ERROR: ... got Bool\n  [host]     CONTROL  Term(5)          => Bool(true)\n\nThe same question about the same value gets \"ground\" from one owner and a hard\ntype error from the other. The CONTROL is the point: they agree exactly when the\ncarrier happens to be Value::Term, i.e. the host answers BY CARRIER, not by\ncontent.\n\nSTATUS TODAY: the carrier divergence is driven (above). What is still LATENT is\nreaching it from the phases named above -- no .anthill file calls `.nonvar(` or\n`.ground(` on a receiver (measured across stdlib, anthill-stl, examples,\nanthill-todo), so no macro body or unfolded body hits it yet.\n\nWHAT TO DECIDE WHILE FIXING: value_is_ground takes a &Substitution and eval has\nnone. Delegation therefore means using the carrier-neutral VALUE-level check\nwhere σ is not needed (occurrence_has_unbound_var already needs none), not\nthreading a σ that does not exist. Whether the reflect API stays two-valued is a\nseparate call: Bool is defensible for an API that can SEE every carrier -- the\ndelay is a resolver concern -- but it must not be Bool BECAUSE it cannot see.\n\nRELATED: WI-535 migrated this bridge off bare TermId for other operations and\nnamed `nonvar/ground` in its scope; this is the part that did not move.\n\nTHREE FURTHER FINDINGS (2026-08-04), each measured, which change the shape of\nthe fix.\n\n1. THE `kb` PARAMETER IS DISPATCH CEREMONY, NOT A DEPENDENCY. Every reflect host\nfn that takes a KB receiver IGNORES it:\n\n     `let [_kb, ...]` (ignored):  11        `let [kb, ...]` (used):  0\n     uses of `interp.kb()` instead:  64\n\nThe KB comes from the interpreter's ambient context. The parameter exists only so\nthe operation DOT-DISPATCHES -- the receiver of a dot call is the first param, so\n`operation nonvar(kb: KB, x: Term)` is what makes `kb.nonvar(x)` a member call.\nConsequence for this ticket: delegating to the carrier-neutral predicate threads\nNOTHING; the member stays a receiver-shaped wrapper and `kb` stays ceremony. Note\nalso that the parameter which creates the 059 name collision is the one the\nimplementation discards.\n\n2. THE COLLISION IS NOT LATENT -- IT BITES IN VALUE POSITION. Measured:\n\n     import anthill.reflect.nonvar ... operation driver() -> Bool = nonvar(1)\n       => REFUSED: \"`nonvar` is a member of sort KB, not in scope as a bare name\n          here; call it qualified as `KB.nonvar(...)` or via a receiver\"\n     CONTROL, the same builtin in GOAL position (a rule body)   => works\n     fully qualified `anthill.reflect.nonvar(1)` in a body      => SAME REFUSAL\n\nSo the 1-ary resolver builtin is reachable as a GOAL and unreachable from an\nOPERATION BODY, and the diagnostic sends the author to the 2-ary member, which is\nnot what was asked for.\n\nNOT ESTABLISHED, and worth pinning first: the fully-qualified row. The name was\nwritten `anthill.reflect.nonvar` and still drew the member's diagnostic. Either\nthe qualified path genuinely resolves onto `anthill.reflect.KB.nonvar`, or the\nWI-565 hint is misattributed from the SHORT name. Those are different bugs --\none is resolution, one is a diagnostic -- and the fix differs.\n\n3. CONVERGENCE: PASS KB IN NEITHER, NOT IN BOTH (user's framing). Passing it in\nboth is out -- the param is ignored, and adding it to the resolver form would put\nceremony in rule bodies, the one place it is pure noise. Passing it in neither\nconverges the two onto ONE NAME and ONE ARITY, which is what \"one owner\" wants\nanyway. THE OBSTACLE, measured: `bare_bodied_bool_relation` (resolve.rs:6234)\nroutes a bare bodied Bool op used as a goal to `eq(goal, true)` -- but BAILS for a\nBUILTIN or a BODY-LESS op, and both of these are exactly that. So convergence is\nnot free: it needs a verdict on whether one name may be both a goal-level builtin\nand a value-returning operation.\n\nCORRECTION to this ticket's own framing, from the user: CARRIER-NEUTRAL DOES NOT\nMEAN STORE-FREE. A neutral `Value` still has a `Value::Term { id }` variant, so a\nreader accepting every carrier still receives TermIds and still needs the store\nfor those; only `Value::Node` is self-describing. The rule is therefore not \"drop\nthe KB\" but \"DO NOT FORCE THE TermId CARRIER -- use the store only for the carrier\nthat needs it.\"\n\nACCEPTANCE: one owner per question -- KB.nonvar/KB.ground answer by the same\ncarrier-neutral predicate the resolver uses, not by a second TermId-only\nderivation. A test in anthill-stl drives each on a Value::Node carrier and on a\nnon-Term scalar, asserting an ANSWER rather than a TypeMismatch; and a control\nasserts the two owners agree on a plain Term, which they do today (so the\ncontrol passes before and after -- say so at the site). cargo-test green via\nrustland/scripts/test.sh."), context: none, acceptance: [ToolPasses(tool: "cargo-test", params: none)], depends_on: some(value: nil), generates: none, requires_capability: none, status: Delivered(agent: "claude", at: "2026-08-04T09:06:59Z"))

fact Feedback(workitem: "WI-982", author: "user", content: "DELIVERED (2026-08-04). One question, one owner; one name, one arity, both phases.\n\nTHERE WERE THREE OWNERS, NOT TWO. The ticket named the resolver and\nreflect/builtins.rs. reflect/bridge.rs had a third -- KbBridge::nonvar / ground,\nwhose comment read \"Mirrors the core resolver's `value_is_ground`\" and did not.\nBoth holes MEASURED, by putting each deleted body back under the new test:\n\n  KbBridge::ground   Value::Entity{ red, [unbound] }  => true    (resolver: false)\n  KbBridge::nonvar   Value::Node(Expr::Var(_))        => true    (resolver: false)\n\nNeither is a TypeMismatch. A WRONG ANSWER, silently. `ground` had no\nEntity/Tuple arm, so a compound with unbound children fell to `_ => true`;\n`nonvar` had no Value::Node arm and did the same thing one variant over.\n\nFINDING 2's UNESTABLISHED ROW -- BOTH OPTIONS REFUTED. The ticket offered a\ndichotomy: \"either the qualified path genuinely resolves onto\nanthill.reflect.KB.nonvar, or the WI-565 hint is misattributed from the SHORT\nname.\" It is a third thing, and a sibling builtin was the discriminator:\n\n  `anthill.reflect.qualified_name(1)` in an operation body\n    => \"type mismatch in qualified_name.s (op-arg): expected Symbol, got Int64\"\n\nThat call TYPED. So the qualified path resolves correctly and the typer has a\ndeclaration to type it against. `nonvar` differed because `anthill.reflect.nonvar`\nWAS DECLARED NOWHERE -- the symbol existed only because register_builtin_tag mints\none for an undeclared name. A tag dispatches a GOAL; it does not type a CALL. So\nthe value-position call fell to the typer's path-3 unknown-functor edge, and the\nWI-565 refinement -- which keys on the SHORT name -- found KB.nonvar and named it.\nResolution was never wrong; the misattributed hint was the second-order symptom\nof a missing declaration.\n\nCensus, because the class matters more than the instance: of the 25 registered\nbuiltin tags, `nonvar` and `ground` were the ONLY two that were an operation in\nintent and declared nowhere. Every other reflect tag has its namespace-level\n`operation`; is_entity_of is a rule, ho_apply an entity, find_dictionary the\n`requires` desugaring target, neq/lt/gt/... spec members. The class is closed --\nno follow-up ticket.\n\nFINDING 3's VERDICT: YES, AND THE STDLIB ALREADY ANSWERS IT EIGHT TIMES. \"whether\none name may be both a goal-level builtin and a value-returning operation\" --\nqualified_name, short_name, lookup_symbol, scope, kind, not, field_access and\nresolve_sort_instantiation_param are each DECLARED and TAGGED today.\n`not(query: Term) -> Bool` is the exact shape: 1-ary, -> Bool, body-less, tagged.\nThe obstacle the ticket named is not one: bare_bodied_bool_relation bails for a\nbuiltin precisely because a builtin goal never needs it -- it dispatches on its\ntag. Convergence was free, so I took the framing recorded in the ticket: PASS KB\nIN NEITHER. KB.nonvar / KB.ground are gone.\n\nWHAT LANDED\n  stdlib  `operation nonvar(x: Term) -> Bool` / `ground(x: Term) -> Bool` at\n          namespace level; both members deleted from `sort KB`.\n  core    value_is_unbound_var / value_is_ground are `pub` -- THE one owner --\n          plus value_is_ground_no_subst, which is where the sigma question lands:\n          eval has no ambient sigma (Interpreter's SubstArena holds first-class\n          Value::Substitution DATA, not a pending resolution state), so the EMPTY\n          sigma is the truth, not a stand-in for a missing one. ONE named entry\n          point rather than an empty sigma conjured at each call site -- a second\n          predicate is exactly how these owners drifted apart.\n  stl     nonvar_op / ground_op, 1-ary, delegating; KbBridge's two deleted.\n\nTHE COMPILER NOW ENFORCES IT. The `KB` trait is GENERATED from reflect.anthill\n(anthill-stl/build.rs, emit_only) and KbBridge implements it, so a fourth copy\ncannot be added to bridge.rs without the trait declaring it, and the trait cannot\ndeclare it without the sort. Deleting the two members deleted the two trait\nmethods; the build proved it.\n\n059 R4 GOES TO ZERO. WI-981 measured exactly 2 no-override captures, and they were\nthese two members. Clause 3 now bites nothing in the corpus and lands with no\nmigration; proposal updated (61/38 in the class table, 2 -> 0 in both tables). The\nverdict was neither of the two the count anticipated (rename / exempt as\nintentional): the CAPTURED names were the ones worth keeping, so the members went.\n\nTESTS, and what each measures\n  nonvar_and_ground_answer_by_content_not_carrier -- 9 carriers x 2 questions x 2\n  doors. MUTATION-VERIFIED three ways: with the old expect_term body every\n  non-control row is a TypeMismatch; with the deleted KbBridge::ground body only\n  the Entity row fails; with the deleted KbBridge::nonvar body only the Node row\n  fails. The two Value::Term rows are labelled CONTROL at the site -- they pass\n  before and after, and that IS the finding: the owners agreed exactly on the one\n  carrier the host could see.\n    Also stated at the site so the resolver rows are not over-read: after the\n    change both doors reach ONE predicate, so they are no longer an independent\n    second opinion -- they are the assertion that the doors stay wired to the same\n    owner, and they fail the moment either grows its own derivation again. Before\n    the change they WERE independent, which is how the divergence was found.\n  nonvar_and_ground_are_callable_from_an_operation_body -- the row the ticket found\n  unreachable, DRIVEN rather than loaded: both spellings it measured (bare via\n  import, fully qualified) are CALLED and their values asserted, so a name\n  resolving to nothing fails here instead of passing quietly.\n  kb_nonvar_and_ground_classify_terms is deleted but not lost -- it is the two\n  CONTROL rows above; a comment at its old site says so.\n  Goal position is unchanged and has real controls: the six resolve.rs\n  nonvar_*/ground_* tests, wi670_floundered_rule_test, and the stdlib's own\n  typing.anthill:48 `nonvar(?x)` guard all stay green.\n\nFull workspace green via scripts/test.sh. scaland unaffected -- it registers the\nsame 1-ary tags and has no eval-time reflect host fns. docs/status/wi-047.md still\nnames KB.nonvar/KB.ground; left alone deliberately, it is a dated delivery record.\n\nNOT DONE, DELIBERATELY, NO TICKET FILED. expect_term still forces the Value::Term\ncarrier for sort_as_term / can_be_sort / term_as_sort. Same CLASS, different\nquestion, and I have no measurement that it bites -- nothing routes a Value::Node\nto them today. The 9 other reflect host fns keep their ignored `_kb`: their\nquestion IS about the KB, so the receiver is the right API shape whatever the\nimplementation happens to read.", at: "2026-08-04T09:07:01Z")
